  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfternoon Update: The environment continues to favor stratiform\nrainfall for the southwestern Adirondacks through the rest of \nthis afternoon. Elsewhere across eastern New York and western\nNew England, light isolated to scattered rain showers continue \nthrough this evening. For locations north and west of Albany \ntonight, there could be a rumble of thunder but based on the \nlatest forecast guidance and environment conditions, the chances\nare low (less than 20%). Breezy conditions are still in store \noutside of scattered rain showers for tonight and into tomorrow \nafternoon before a cold front moves through. The rest of the \nforecast is on track, see the previous discussion below for more\ndetails. \n\nPrevious Discussion: Latest trends in guidance indicating \nshowers developing a bit earlier than previously forecast, as a \nlead disturbance approaching from the Great Lakes moves in. Will\nmention likely to categorical PoPs developing from NW to SE for\nareas mainly along and north of I-90 later this afternoon to \nearly evening. Humidity levels will noticeably increase today, \nwith dewpoints rising into the 60s. Showers with some embedded \nconvective downpours are expected to then spread south/east \nacross the rest of the area later this evening into tonight \nahead of a cold front. Latest guidance (including REFS/HREF) \nstill indicating limited instability with CAPE < 500 J/Kg from \naround Albany north and west. However, with increasing 0-6 km \nshear of 35-45 kt a few storms could produce gusty winds. It is \nlikely storms will not grow tall enough to become severe though.\nSPC continues to indicate a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) of \nsevere storms for a small area including the Mohawk Valley and \nSW Adirondacks for a few possible rogue stronger gusts. Severe \nprobs remain low though due to limited instability and storms \nmoving in primarily after dark. Deep layer moisture does \nincrease fairly substantially, with PWAT anomalies now forecast \nto be +2 to +3 STDEV from the NAEFS. So brief torrential \ndownpours could occur with any convective elements, but the \noverall threat for flash flooding is low due to expected fast \nstorm motion and dry antecedent conditions. Rainfall amounts \nwill be generally less than a half inch, except > 1\" in the W/SW\nAdirondacks due to upslope enhancement. Chances for showers \nwill wane by daybreak as the cold front moves through.\n\nWith strengthening SW winds aloft later today into tonight \n(35-45 kt at 850 mb), occasional gusts of 20-30 mph will occur \nin favored S-N oriented valleys and southern facing higher \nterrain slopes. As the cold front pushes through early Thu \nmorning, winds will shift to the W-NW with more widespread gusts\nof 20-30 mph expected during the daylight hours Thu in a \ndeveloping deeper mixing environment. Temperatures will still \nbe warm (highs 80-85F in Hudson Valley) due to deeper mixing, \nalthough humidity levels will start to drop from north to south \nlater in the day. Dry conditions are expected through the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter a seasonably cool and dry Fri-Sat period, temperatures \nwill warm above normal Sun into early next week as high pressure\nmoves off the coast and a warm southerly flow develops. It will\nalso become more humid during this time. Isolated/scattered \nshowers/T-storms may occur, especially on Tue as a cold front \nis expected to move east across the region.",
